---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Lawrenceville, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or storm water in Lawrenceville, GA 30043, 30044, 30045, 30046. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Lawrenceville" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can
      about the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Lawrenceville</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Lawrenceville</h2>
    <p>
      Lawrenceville is the county seat, and it is bigger than the downtown most
      people picture. Downtown has two centers that locals name separately, the
      Square and the Lawn. The rest of the city runs out from there: Collins
      Hill, Sugarloaf Mills, the Old Norcross Road side, and a lot of lake and
      pond neighborhoods built in the late 1980s and 1990s.
    </p>
    <p>
      Many of those houses are 25 to 40 years old. The water heater, the supply
      lines, and the washer hoses in them are showing it. So the usual call is a
      leak, not a flood. It is a water heater that let go, a soft spot in the
      kitchen floor, or a stain on the ceiling.
    </p>
    <p>
      The south and west sides of the city are different. Wolf Creek and Pew
      Creek run through that part of town, and during Hurricane Helene in 2024
      county officials watched both of them for flooding. The Collins Hill and
      Sugarloaf side did not get the same attention. If you live near a creek,
      water from outside is a real possibility, and it is handled differently
      from a pipe leak.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is under the house changes where the water goes. Older houses near
      downtown more often sit over a crawl space. Many lake and hillside streets
      have basements. On a slab, water spreads across the floor and soaks up into
      the walls.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or send
      the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and how
      long it has been there. Tell us if the house has a basement, a crawl space,
      or a slab. If you are not sure, say so. That is enough to start.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage too. Trees came down across Lawrenceville in a
      January 2022 winter storm, and in 2018 one went through a living room. A
      damaged roof can leak into the attic for days before the stain shows up on
      the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>Lawrenceville is a lot of different places. These are the ones people name.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Downtown.</strong> Around the Square and the Lawn. It has older
      houses and newer apartments and town homes, and many people here rent.
      Court is at the Gwinnett Justice and Administration Center on Langley
      Drive, not the old courthouse on the Square.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Collins Hill.</strong> Mostly 1990s brick two-story houses.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Sugarloaf Mills and Sugarloaf Parkway.</strong> Houses and some
      town homes from the 1990s and 2000s.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The lake neighborhoods.</strong> Edgewater was built between 1989
      and 2000. Chandler Pond and Knollwood Lakes are from the 1990s. River
      Colony is from the late 1980s. Great River at Tribble Mill is newer, from
      the 2000s.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Old Norcross Road, and the south and west side.</strong> An older
      corridor with a mix of houses. This is the part of the city that floods
      before the rest.
    </p>
    <p>ZIP codes here include 30043, 30044, 30045, and 30046.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Lawrenceville</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the late 1980s through the 1990s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Old pipes.</strong> Some houses from the late 1980s and early 1990s
        may still have gray plastic pipe that is known to fail. See the question below.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Creek water.</strong> In south and west Lawrenceville, heavy rain
        can push water out of a creek and into a yard, a garage, or a house.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm damage.</strong> A tree or wind takes off shingles. Rain gets
        in through the roof, a window, or a door.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Lawrenceville homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>
          Water is coming up from the street or around my water meter. Who do I
          call?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Call Gwinnett County's Department of Water Resources. It has run the
          water system in Lawrenceville since December 2020. If water is also
          inside your house, shut off the main valve, take photos, and call us
          about the wet part inside.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          Creek water got into my garage or yard. Is that the same as a burst
          pipe?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          No. Water from outside is dirty water, and it is often covered
          differently in an insurance policy. Do not walk through it, and stay
          away from wet outlets. Take photos of the water line on the walls and
          call. Ask your insurer whether your policy covers outside water.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>My house was built in the late 1980s. Should I worry about the pipes?</dt>
        <dd>
          Look at the pipes where they come out of the wall near the water
          heater. If they are gray plastic, that may be polybutylene, which was
          used in many houses until the mid-1990s and is known to fail. You
          cannot tell by looking at the neighborhood, so ask a plumber about your
          house. Do not assume it either way.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          A tree fell on my roof last night and it is raining inside. Do I wait
          for the roof first?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          No. Water that gets into the attic soaks the insulation and then the
          ceiling. Take photos, put buckets down, and call about the water now.
          Fix the roof as soon as you can, and tell your insurer about both.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>I rent downtown and the ceiling is leaking. Who do I call?</dt>
        <dd>
          Call your landlord or property manager first, because they own the
          building and often have their own insurance and a company they use.
          Take photos of the leak and of anything of yours that got wet. Your own
          renters insurance covers your belongings, not the building. Tell us what
          you know when you call.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer backup. Say which one it is on
      the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="lawrenceville" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/dacula">Dacula</a> ·
      <a href="/hamilton-mill">Hamilton Mill</a> ·
      <a href="/grayson">Grayson</a> ·
      <a href="/snellville">Snellville</a> ·
      <a href="/duluth">Duluth</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
